,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.502622516,"data":"1. Contact your landlord or property manager: contact your landlord or property manager and inform them of your intention to evict your tenant. Provide them with all the necessary information, including the reason for eviction, the date of eviction, and any other relevant details.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.503411941,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.504261285,"data":"2. Provide notice: provide notice to your tenant of your intention to evict them. This notice should be sent via email, text message, or any other method that your landlord or property manager prefers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.505109525,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.505941598,"data":"3. Follow up: after providing notice, follow up with your tenant to ensure that they have received the notice and that they have no objections to the eviction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.506863895,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":82.507687673,"data":"4. Take legal action: if your tenant does not respond to your eviction notice, you can take legal action to evict them. You can file a lawsuit in court and seek to evict them from the property.\n"}
