,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.40358199,"data":"2. Gender pay gap: women are paid less than men for the same work, which perpetuates the gender pay gap. This disparity in pay is a clear indication that women are not as valuable as men in the workplace.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.404579674,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.405402308,"data":"3. Lack of education: women are often underrepresented in fields that require advanced degrees, such as science and engineering. This lack of education and experience can lead to a perception that women are not as capable as men.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.406280724,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.407133057,"data":"4. Social expectations: society often expects women to be more nurturing and caring than men, which can lead to a perception that women are not as capable of taking care of themselves and their families.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.407996604,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.409141955,"data":"5. Cultural norms: cultural norms and traditions can also contribute to the perception of women as inferior. For example, in some cultures, women are expected to be submissive and obedient to men, which can lead to a perception that women are not capable of making decisions or taking risks\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":804.41035921,"data":"\n"}
