,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.64611647,"data":"Instruction:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.647321809,"data":"Explain overfitting in machine learning to a high school student in simple terms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.648545944,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.64984227,"data":"Response A:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.650885681,"data":"Overfitting is when a machine learning model learns too much from the training data and fails to learn the underlying patterns in the data. It happens when the model becomes too complex and starts to memorize the training data, rather than learning from it. This means that the model will perform well on the training data but poorly on new, unseen data. Overfitting can lead to poor performance on both training and test data, and can also lead to overfitting the model itself. To avoid overfitting, it's important to use regularization techniques such as regularization, dropout, or early stopping to prevent the model from becoming too complex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.651731543,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.652536219,"data":"Response B:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.653361392,"data":"Overfitting is when a machine learning model learns too much from the training data and doesn't learn the underlying patterns in the data. It can make the model perform well on the training data but poorly on new, unseen data. This is because the model has memorized the patterns in the training data and doesn't learn from the data outside of the training set. Overfitting can lead to poor performance on new data and can make it difficult to predict future outcomes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.65420766,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":898.654949373,"data":"Which response is better? Reply with EXACTLY one word: \"A\", \"B\", or \"tie\". Do not explain.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":900.332727345,"data":"🤖 [google/gemini-3.6-flash]: B\n"}
